en_events={
1:('A Shared Countdown','Japanese preparations begin. Both countries now use the same invasion deadline; diplomatic concessions and preparations can alter the shared clock.','Prepare the islands.'),
2:('The Islands Speak After Defeat','Workers and village delegates gather outside the residence. They demand autonomy and a say in decisions made in their name. Ludolf promises reform; the council questions his right to lead it.','We must answer them.'),
3:('The Cabinet Remains, for Now','The council opens hearings on Ludolfs responsibility. He stays in office for 240 days while presenting reforms and gathering support. Dismissal will require an explicit political settlement.','Open the hearings.'),
4:('Who Has the Right to Decide?','The council demands unified military authority. Island delegates demand accountable government. Ludolf argues that replacing a cabinet cannot restore trust by itself. Every charter and appointment now shapes the future.','Let the country hear both sides.'),
5:('The Final Hearing','The hearings have expired. Autonomous sultanates can sponsor a federal mandate for Ludolf; the admirals demand his dismissal and imprisonment. The temporary cabinet must give way to a lasting settlement.','Grant Ludolf a federal mandate.'),
6:('One Line in the Law','Racial restrictions on citizenship and public office are repealed. Delegates insist that elected institutions must enforce the promise beyond Batavia.','Citizenship is not ancestry.'),
7:('A Name on the Appointment List','A district council rejects Batavias nominee and proposes a local official. Ludolf accepts. Indonesian communities and sultanates gain a real role in nominations and public hearings.','Officials must answer to residents.'),
8:('Signatures Under a Shared Country','The cabinet, island representatives and autonomous sultanates sign the federal constitution. Elections and common citizenship replace exclusive colonial appointments.','The Netherlands becomes a shared undertaking.'),
9:('The Seals Change Hands','Military commissioners replace civilian officials and seal archives. Political disagreement becomes evidence of disloyalty. Administration slows as the council consolidates control.','The military controls appointments.'),
10:('An Oath Behind Closed Doors','Officers must swear personal loyalty to the council. Experience no longer guarantees a command. The purges weaken coordination while building a loyal corps.','Loyalty becomes a condition of service.'),
11:('The Regent Arrives','Neither the council nor the civilian cabinet could secure lasting authority. Emma accepts the regency as an arbiter, preserving naval command and opening negotiations on island rights.','The regency must hold the country together.'),
12:('A Charter for the Islands','Emma confirms autonomous rights and community representation. The delegates insist that the charter must bind future monarchs as well as the regent.','The charter binds the crown.'),
13:('Wilhelmina Takes Office','The adult Wilhelmina succeeds Queen Mother Emma. Alongside the state seal, she receives copies of the island charters. The regency ends; the duty to negotiate remains.','Long live Queen Wilhelmina.'),
14:('The Crown and the Law','The new constitution separates the powers of the monarch, civilian institutions and military command, protecting island representation and autonomous charters.','No order stands above the constitution.'),
15:('The State Becomes a Headquarters','Ministries, courts and appointments now answer to the council. Ludolf remains imprisoned. The purges end in complete control of the state by the military elite.','Every institution answers to the council.'),
16:('An Unmarked Envelope','With neither faction able to prevail, Emma privately offers a regency and guarantees for the islands. Ludolf must choose between yielding to an arbiter and continuing the unresolved struggle.','Recognise Emma as regent and arbiter.'),
}
